WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:06.090
Wonder Woman 1984 is a treat for all ages,
but there are many aspects of it that only

00:00:06.090 --> 00:00:07.220
adult viewers grasp.

00:00:07.220 --> 00:00:11.770
From the film's most obscure references to
its uncomfortable moments, these are all the

00:00:11.770 --> 00:00:15.070
things only adults notice in Wonder Woman
1984.

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:21.650
As the title implies, Wonder Woman 1984 picks
up with Diana in the midst of one of Hollywood's

00:00:21.650 --> 00:00:23.690
favorite decades to portray: The 1980s.

00:00:23.690 --> 00:00:28.520
There's a lot to love about the '80s, from
the decade's awesome music to its gleaming

00:00:28.520 --> 00:00:29.850
video stores.

00:00:29.850 --> 00:00:34.690
And anyone who lived through the 1980s will
recognize many real details from that time

00:00:34.690 --> 00:00:38.429
scattered throughout the set of Wonder Woman
1984.

00:00:38.429 --> 00:00:42.929
Sparked by the public's obsession with fitness,
people everywhere were donning spandex and

00:00:42.929 --> 00:00:43.929
leg warmers.

00:00:43.929 --> 00:00:49.149
In its earliest shots of people on the streets
of DC, Wonder Woman 1984 offers a number of

00:00:49.149 --> 00:00:50.799
examples of this look.

00:00:50.799 --> 00:00:54.999
Speaking of fashion, no '80s setting would
be complete with ridiculously over-the-top

00:00:54.999 --> 00:00:58.249
parachute pants and a whole lot of metallic
fabric.

00:00:58.249 --> 00:01:03.359
Steve Trevor shows off a few of these looks
when he and Diana attempt to find him an era-appropriate

00:01:03.359 --> 00:01:04.359
look.

00:01:04.359 --> 00:01:08.730
Even the crosswalk signs in Wonder Woman 1984
are accurate to the time period.

00:01:08.730 --> 00:01:13.370
Before today's modern LEDs were installed
in the early 2000's, the signage did indeed

00:01:13.370 --> 00:01:18.640
display the words "Walk/Don't Walk," rather
than the current symbols representing a person

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:19.640
in mid-stride.

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:25.410
Wonder Woman 1984 gets a whole lot right about
the 1980s, but there is still one important

00:01:25.410 --> 00:01:28.500
plot detail that it manages to get majorly
wrong.

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:32.770
This error can be spotted at the beginning
of Max Lord's big finale speech.

00:01:32.770 --> 00:01:37.500
After successfully taking over the world's
broadcasting signals using secret government

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:42.190
tech, Lord is able to splash his face across
the screens of unwitting bystanders everywhere.

00:01:42.190 --> 00:01:46.730
No matter where Diana turns, his face is visible,
urging the people of the world to wish for

00:01:46.730 --> 00:01:49.810
what they want and see their wildest dreams
come true.

00:01:49.810 --> 00:01:53.480
The only problem with this super-villainous
scheme is that those screens broadcasting

00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:57.210
Lord's face include television and computer
displays.

00:01:57.210 --> 00:02:01.690
This scene takes place decades before the
internet or streaming services became publicly

00:02:01.690 --> 00:02:02.690
available.

00:02:02.690 --> 00:02:07.130
In fact, the concept of a home computer was
considered incredibly novel and exotic at

00:02:07.130 --> 00:02:11.950
the time, as Apple had just started selling
their first generation of Macintosh computers

00:02:11.950 --> 00:02:13.180
that January.

00:02:13.180 --> 00:02:18.159
This sort of all-encompassing broadcast wasn't
just impossible at the time — it was basically

00:02:18.159 --> 00:02:19.159
unthinkable.

00:02:19.159 --> 00:02:22.170
"This is a highly sophisticated office computer."

00:02:22.170 --> 00:02:26.909
That this blooper wasn't caught by the production
team prior to release just goes to show how

00:02:26.909 --> 00:02:29.629
easy it is to take technology for granted.

00:02:30.799 --> 00:02:34.799
Adults can't help but roll their eyes when
one of Max Lord's last business investors,

00:02:34.799 --> 00:02:39.450
Simon Stagg, threatens to report him to the
Federal Trade Commission if Max doesn't return

00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:42.220
his investment money within 48 hours.

00:02:42.220 --> 00:02:46.510
This sort of "race against the clock" trope
is favored by film and television producers,

00:02:46.510 --> 00:02:50.659
because it provides a sense of urgency to
the plot without being too restrictive.

00:02:50.659 --> 00:02:53.980
The 48-hour timeline is a particularly popular
choice.

00:02:53.980 --> 00:02:56.159
"You have 48 hours starting on my mark."

00:02:56.159 --> 00:02:58.939
"You have 48 hours to get my money."

00:02:58.939 --> 00:03:00.769
"In 48 hours."

00:03:00.769 --> 00:03:05.650
"You have 20 seconds" "You have 48 hours."

00:03:05.650 --> 00:03:11.569
The 1982 action-comedy movie 48 Hrs., starring
Nick Nolte and Eddie Murphy, literally calls

00:03:11.569 --> 00:03:14.629
itself out for using this cliche in its very
title.

00:03:14.629 --> 00:03:19.670
We can't blame the filmmakers for using this
reliable plot point, but it does get repetitive.

00:03:19.670 --> 00:03:24.110
Younger viewers may find Max Lord's conflict
engaging, but more mature audiences might

00:03:24.110 --> 00:03:26.159
find it to be more than a little bit predictable.

00:03:27.249 --> 00:03:31.769
The technology Max Lord uses to take over
the planet's airwaves in Wonder Woman 1984

00:03:31.769 --> 00:03:36.500
may be the stuff of science fiction, but the
parent program mentioned in the movie as its

00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:38.980
comparison was actually entirely real.

00:03:38.980 --> 00:03:43.469
The 1980s marked the height of the Cold War,
when the United States and the Soviet Union

00:03:43.469 --> 00:03:48.370
were attempting to one-up each other while
maintaining a public pretense of peace.

00:03:48.370 --> 00:03:52.949
Secret government programs proliferated during
this era, one of which was known as the Strategic

00:03:52.949 --> 00:03:59.840
Defense Initiative, or SDI, a missile defense
system proposed by Ronald Reagan in 1983 as

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:02.889
an attempt to render nuclear weapons obsolete.

00:04:02.889 --> 00:04:08.029
Nicknamed the "Star Wars program," the SDI
proposed to utilize state-of-the-art technology

00:04:08.029 --> 00:04:13.919
and weapons like lasers, satellites, and ground
and space-based missile systems to neutralize

00:04:13.919 --> 00:04:16.389
incoming threats, like nuclear missiles.

00:04:16.389 --> 00:04:18.310
It was officially scrapped in 1993.

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:24.340
Kristen Wiig does an excellent job portraying
the painfully shy and awkward character of

00:04:24.340 --> 00:04:29.980
Barbara Minerva, who transforms from an introverted
wallflower into the empowered apex predator

00:04:29.980 --> 00:04:31.130
known as Cheetah.

00:04:31.130 --> 00:04:35.820
However, Barbara's character arc might seem
remarkably familiar to older audience members

00:04:35.820 --> 00:04:37.170
who have seen more movies.

00:04:37.170 --> 00:04:41.330
That's because Barbara's story revolves on
the axis of a heavily played-out trope — the

00:04:41.330 --> 00:04:45.920
one where the disregarded nerdy girl gets
a makeover, and suddenly transforms into a

00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:47.360
popular hottie.

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:51.630
Just look at My Fair Lady, Grease, The Princess
Diaries, and She's All That.

00:04:51.630 --> 00:04:56.530
Indeed, this plot point has been seen countless
times before, and its use tends to marginalize

00:04:56.530 --> 00:04:59.590
the character in question far more than it
empowers them.

00:04:59.590 --> 00:05:03.760
To put it plainly, no one is surprised to
see the ugly duckling become the swan any

00:05:03.760 --> 00:05:07.840
longer — especially because the duckling
is pretty much always already attractive.

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:11.390
"Okay now take your glasses off, let that
hair down, and act like you don't know how

00:05:11.390 --> 00:05:12.390
pretty you are."

00:05:13.390 --> 00:05:18.480
In Wonder Woman 1984, Diana unwittingly uses
the coveted Dreamstone to bring her lost love

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:22.740
Steve Trevor back from the dead — but he
doesn't return in the way you'd expect.

00:05:22.740 --> 00:05:27.340
Rather than magically resurrecting his body,
the Dreamstone inserts Steve Trevor's consciousness

00:05:27.340 --> 00:05:31.510
into the body of another man living near Diana
in Washington, DC.

00:05:31.510 --> 00:05:36.410
To minimize audience confusion, the "Handsome
Man," as he's credited, is shown briefly at

00:05:36.410 --> 00:05:40.850
the start of his possession, then his likeness
switches to Steve Trevor's.

00:05:40.850 --> 00:05:45.190
It's possible this was intended as some sort
of metaphor about only having eyes for your

00:05:45.190 --> 00:05:46.190
true love.

00:05:46.190 --> 00:05:50.250
This is something younger audiences might
be able to buy into a little more easily.

00:05:50.250 --> 00:05:55.040
But this magical body-snatching comes off
as more problematic than romantic for adults.

00:05:55.040 --> 00:05:59.620
Steve Trevor doesn't know who Handsome Man
is, and disrupts his entire life with his

00:05:59.620 --> 00:06:00.620
presence.

00:06:00.620 --> 00:06:04.330
What happens to his job, his social life,
and all other aspects of his existence when

00:06:04.330 --> 00:06:05.770
he suddenly becomes Steve?

00:06:05.770 --> 00:06:10.950
Wouldn't the poor man be terrified when suddenly
coming to, after Steve forfeits his parasitic

00:06:10.950 --> 00:06:11.950
existence?

00:06:11.950 --> 00:06:16.370
Also, one can't help but wonder what the implications
are of being intimate with someone's body

00:06:16.370 --> 00:06:19.160
when they have no presence of mind to provide
consent.

00:06:19.160 --> 00:06:21.571
At the very least, it's unnerving.

00:06:21.571 --> 00:06:26.750
Wonder Woman 1984 is a superhero movie, so
there are always going to be some weird physics

00:06:26.750 --> 00:06:27.750
involved.

00:06:27.750 --> 00:06:29.350
These people are superheroes, after all.

00:06:29.350 --> 00:06:30.630
Can Diana lasso lightning?

00:06:30.630 --> 00:06:31.630
Sure!

00:06:31.630 --> 00:06:32.630
Why not?

00:06:32.630 --> 00:06:36.100
But the film is also loaded with unrealistic
depictions of physics and technology that

00:06:36.100 --> 00:06:39.340
are hard to ignore, especially for a jaded
adult.

00:06:39.340 --> 00:06:44.200
For example, when Steve insists on flying
a modern plane, he does so without issue,

00:06:44.200 --> 00:06:48.340
despite the massive differences between planes
he flew during World War I and the modern

00:06:48.340 --> 00:06:49.690
jet he and Diana commandeer.

00:06:49.690 --> 00:06:53.170
"The way I fly, they will never find us."

00:06:53.170 --> 00:06:58.130
And when Diana manages to make the jet magically
invisible, it also simultaneously disappears

00:06:58.130 --> 00:07:02.500
from radar — but just because an object
becomes invisible to the naked eye doesn't

00:07:02.500 --> 00:07:07.050
mean it also becomes invisible to radar, because
the mass of the plane is still there to reflect

00:07:07.050 --> 00:07:08.050
the radar waves.

00:07:08.050 --> 00:07:12.420
Then again, she made the plane invisible,
so we can't nitpick this one too much.

00:07:12.420 --> 00:07:15.050
Maybe it's all part of her invisibility magic.

00:07:15.050 --> 00:07:19.750
But there's also the fact that at high altitudes,
it gets pretty hard to breathe without oxygen.

00:07:19.750 --> 00:07:22.850
That's a little harder to explain away for
human man Steve.

00:07:22.850 --> 00:07:24.550
"Put the mask back on.

00:07:24.550 --> 00:07:25.920
That's an order, Marine.

00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:26.950
I can't breathe."

00:07:26.950 --> 00:07:31.150
But Diana and Steve chat away unassisted,
as though they're toddling down the highway,

00:07:31.150 --> 00:07:35.390
rather than speeding through the air thousands
of feet up at hundreds of miles an hour.

00:07:35.390 --> 00:07:40.120
Likewise, the final battle between Wonder
Woman and Cheetah defies the rules of electricity

00:07:40.120 --> 00:07:41.270
to the point of silliness.

00:07:41.270 --> 00:07:45.990
The two women are impervious to thousands
of volts of electricity in ways that can only

00:07:45.990 --> 00:07:50.670
be explained with comic book logic -- and
again, this is a comic book movie, so that's

00:07:50.670 --> 00:07:51.670
fair game.

00:07:51.670 --> 00:07:56.030
Then, suddenly, those excuses stop mattering
at all when the live wire Cheetah swings from

00:07:56.030 --> 00:08:00.220
without any apparent trouble contacts the
water she's immersed in, and becomes the source

00:08:00.220 --> 00:08:01.220
of her defeat.

00:08:02.550 --> 00:08:05.250
Professional wrestling is beloved by people
of all ages.

00:08:05.250 --> 00:08:09.450
But younger fans likely miss the real-life
reference planted in the midst of Wonder Woman

00:08:09.450 --> 00:08:10.670
1984.

00:08:10.670 --> 00:08:15.300
While visiting the home of a man with important
information regarding the Dreamstone, Diana

00:08:15.300 --> 00:08:17.830
examines a magazine lying near his front door.

00:08:17.830 --> 00:08:23.180
It's the August 1984 issue of Pro-Wrestling
Illustrated, featuring Jerry "the King" Lawler

00:08:23.180 --> 00:08:24.470
on the front cover.

00:08:24.470 --> 00:08:28.980
As it turns out, this particular issue really
does exist, and it really was in print at

00:08:28.980 --> 00:08:29.980
the time.

00:08:29.980 --> 00:08:34.840
This was excitedly pointed out by Lawler himself
on Twitter, after Stuart Schreck of DC Comics

00:08:34.840 --> 00:08:36.140
brought it to his attention.

00:08:36.140 --> 00:08:41.621
It might seem random to feature a pro-wrestling
themed magazine in Wonder Woman 1984, but

00:08:41.621 --> 00:08:46.279
at the time, sports entertainment had reached
a pinnacle of unparalleled popularity.

00:08:46.279 --> 00:08:50.990
The 1980s were a huge decade for professional
wrestling in particular, to the point that

00:08:50.990 --> 00:08:53.680
it is often referred to as the sport's "Golden
Age."

00:08:53.680 --> 00:08:57.980
Offering a nod to this aspect of the decade
is something many grown-up viewers of Wonder

00:08:57.980 --> 00:09:01.560
Woman 1984 no doubt appreciate.

00:09:01.560 --> 00:09:07.570
In the DCEU, Asteria is a celebrated champion
of the Amazons who selflessly sacrificed herself

00:09:07.570 --> 00:09:12.150
in battle so the rest of her sisters could
flee to the safety of what would become Themyscira.

00:09:12.150 --> 00:09:17.540
This fallen hero is remembered as the "Golden
Warrior" for her courageous heart and legendary

00:09:17.540 --> 00:09:18.540
armor.

00:09:18.540 --> 00:09:23.199
To honor Asteria, a golden statue of her likeness
was erected in Themyscira's coliseum.

00:09:23.199 --> 00:09:27.630
The Amazonian athletic competition shown in
the beginning of the film is also held as

00:09:27.630 --> 00:09:30.940
a way to remember and reflect upon Asteria's
legacy.

00:09:30.940 --> 00:09:35.860
In real life, Asteria was a name given to
several prominent figures in Greek mythology

00:09:35.860 --> 00:09:37.580
meaning "starry one."

00:09:37.580 --> 00:09:42.180
Interestingly, this particular strand of mythology
portrays Asteria as eventually transforming

00:09:42.180 --> 00:09:45.250
into an island to escape Zeus' advances.

00:09:45.250 --> 00:09:49.230
Adult viewers will pick up on how reminiscent
this is of her origin story regarding the

00:09:49.230 --> 00:09:53.690
island of Themyscira, and how it echoes the
Amazons' desire to separate themselves from

00:09:53.690 --> 00:09:54.690
the world of men.

00:09:54.690 --> 00:09:59.940
Culture and technology may have changed, but
tales of heroes and villains remain timeless.

00:09:59.940 --> 00:10:04.060
Considering she fell in battle and was never
heard from again, the Amazons naturally assumed

00:10:04.060 --> 00:10:06.280
that Asteria died defending them.

00:10:06.280 --> 00:10:10.430
This assumption was reinforced when Diana
went on a mission to find the fallen warrior

00:10:10.430 --> 00:10:15.029
and was only able to track down Asteria's
Golden Eagle armor, which she winds up using

00:10:15.029 --> 00:10:18.220
in her final face-off against Cheetah and
Max Lord.

00:10:18.220 --> 00:10:24.180
However, Wonder Woman 1984's mid-credits scene
reveals that Asteria is very much alive, secretly

00:10:24.180 --> 00:10:29.040
living among normal humans and saving the
occasional baby when the opportunity presents

00:10:29.040 --> 00:10:30.040
itself.

00:10:30.040 --> 00:10:34.130
Although younger viewers may not recognize
the stunning brunette playing Asteria in this

00:10:34.130 --> 00:10:36.020
scene, adults undoubtedly will: She's the
one and only Lynda Carter.

00:10:36.020 --> 00:10:40.290
Carter made a name for herself playing Diana
Prince on the classic Wonder Woman television

00:10:40.290 --> 00:10:46.820
series, which originally aired on ABC and
CBS from 1975 to 1979.

00:10:46.820 --> 00:10:50.600
Despite the fact that she's been portraying
Amazon warriors for quite some time, Carter

00:10:50.600 --> 00:10:55.580
still manages to dazzle new viewers in 2020
and beyond just as she did all the way back

00:10:55.580 --> 00:10:56.580
in the '70s.

00:10:56.580 --> 00:11:01.290
It will be interesting to see what DC has
planned for the iconic actress: Hopefully,

00:11:01.290 --> 00:11:05.779
there's more to come for her new character
beyond this brief, if thrilling, appearance.

